      {
        "id": "v2-2590f-p35-practice-j18-232713-j18-q-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "For $ax+b=c$, explain how the sign of $a$ and the sign of $c-b$ tell you the sign of $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\dfrac{c-b}{a}$, so $x$ is positive if $a$ and $c-b$ have the same sign, negative if they have opposite signs, and zero if $c-b=0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}ax&=c-b\\\\x&=\\frac{c-b}{a}\\end{align*}$$A quotient is positive when both numbers have the same sign, negative when the signs differ, and zero when $c-b=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
